'''''Blidingia minima''''' is a species of [[seaweed]] in the [[Kornmanniaceae]] family. It was described by [[Johann Harald Kylin|Johann Kylin]] in 1947.<ref name=worms/>

==Description==
The species is {{convert|1|-|10|cm}} long by {{convert|1|-|2|mm}} wide and is light green in colour.<ref>{{cite web|url= http://www.iopan.gda.pl/~wiktor/macroalgae/bli_min.html|title=''Blidingia minima'' (Nageli ex Kutzing) Kylin, 1947|publisher=Svalbard's Macroalgae|access-date=June 7, 2013}}</ref> It [[frond]]s are tubular with it cell arrangement being irregular and {{convert|10|µm}} in diameter. Stellate [[chloroplast]]s also exist. The tubes rise in groups from the center.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.marlin.ac.uk/speciesinformation.php?speciesID=2777|title=A green seaweed - Blidingia minima|publisher=[[The Marine Life Information Network]]|access-date=June 6, 2013}}</ref> It [[Thallus|thalli]] grow as clusters which carry cylindrical and hollow tubes which arise from discoidal cushion.<ref name=seaweedsofalaska>{{cite web|url= http://www.seaweedsofalaska.com/species.asp?SeaweedID=2|title=''Blidingia minima''|publisher=Seaweed of Alaska|access-date=June 6, 2013}}</ref> The tubes are {{convert|1|-|20|mm}} long and {{convert|2|mm}} wide. Thallus cells are {{convert|7|µm}} wide and are angular.<ref>{{cite journal|jstor=3226170|author1=Joel A. Swanson  |author2=Gary L. Floyd  |title=Fine Structure of the Zoospores and Thallus of Blidingia minima|journal=Transactions of the American Microscopical Society|volume=97 | issue = 4|date=October 1978|pages=549–558|doi=10.2307/3226170 }}</ref>

==Distribution==
The type locality of this species is  [[Heligoland|Helgoland, Germany]].<ref>{{AlgaeBase species|name=Blidingia minima|id=33}}</ref> The species is found throughout eastern part of [[Asia]] in such countries as [[China]],  [[Japan]], [[Korea]] and eastern part of [[Russia]].<ref name=seaweedsofalaska/> It is also found in [[Azores]], [[Canada]], [[Norway]], [[South Africa]], [[United States]], and various gulfs, seas and oceans, such as [[Gulf of Maine]] and [[Gulf of Mexico]] and in [[Atlantic Ocean|Atlantic]] and [[Pacific Ocean]]s and [[Wadden Sea]].<ref name=worms>{{cite WoRMS|author=Guiry, Michael D|year=2013|title=''Blidingia minima'' (Nägeli ex Kützing) Kylin, 1947|id=145950|access-date=June 6, 2013}}</ref> Besides this places it have a [[type locality (biology)|type locality]] which is and is also common in [[Ireland]] and [[Great Britain]].<ref>{{cite web|url= http://www.algaebase.org/search/species/detail/?species_id=33|title=''Blidingia minima'' (Nägeli ex Kützing) Kylin, 1947|publisher=[[AlgaeBase]]|access-date=June 6, 2013}}</ref> It was also recorded in [[Queensland]], [[Australia]].<ref>{{cite web|url= http://wetlandinfo.ehp.qld.gov.au/wetlands/ecology/components/species/?blidingia-minima |title=Blidingia minima|publisher=WetlandInfo|website=[[Department of Environment and Heritage Protection (Queensland)|Department of Environment and Heritage Protection]]|date=May 9, 2013|access-date=June 7, 2013}}</ref>

===English distribution===
In England, the species was recorded from [[East Sussex]].<ref>{{cite web|url= http://www.bioimages.org.uk/html/r163211.htm|title=English distribution|publisher=BioImages: The Virtual Field-Guide (UK)|access-date=June 7, 2013}}</ref>

==Habitat==
It is found growing on [[Cobble (geology)|cobble]], [[boulder]]s, [[bedrock]]s, [[barnacle]]s, [[driftwood]] and even on different algae species throughout the semi-protected and protected areas.<ref name=seaweedsofalaska/>

==Ecology==
''Blidingia minima'' is considered to be a food for [[Littorinidae|periwinkles]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.ecomare.nl/en/ecomare-encyclopedie/animals-and-plants/plants/algae-and-seaweed/green-seaweed/blidingia-minima/|archive-url=https://archive.today/20130624232611/http://www.ecomare.nl/en/ecomare-encyclopedie/animals-and-plants/plants/algae-and-seaweed/green-seaweed/blidingia-minima/|url-status=dead|archive-date=June 24, 2013|title=Blidingia minima|publisher=Ecomare|access-date=June 6, 2013}}</ref>

